WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.660
In this video, you find the add-on that turns on Kube-OVN in SUSE Virtualization, and see what it adds: virtual private clouds, subnets, and NAT gateways for virtual machine networks.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:22.910
Under Advanced, Add-ons, Kube-OVN ships as the experimental add-on kubeovn-operator. On this cluster, it is already enabled, and its state is Deploy Successful.

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:25.450
Open its action menu.

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:38.350
On a cluster where the add-on is off, this item reads Enable. Selecting it installs the Kube-OVN operator, which deploys Kube-OVN next to the default cluster network. Here it is already on, so leave the menu.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:44.750
The add-on also adds the Overlay Networks menu.

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:47.760
Select Virtual Private Cloud.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:01:00.390
Kube-OVN starts with the default VPC, ovn-cluster. Its subnet ovn-default and the join subnet are created by Kube-OVN itself. The other VPCs and subnets on this page belong to other workloads in this lab.

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:11.970
From these pages, you create your own VPCs and subnets, connect VPCs with peering, and give them internet access with NAT gateways. The next videos build them step by step.

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:19.824
Kube-OVN is on, with its default VPC and subnets. Next, you build your own VPC with several subnets.
